<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-17" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Introduction Activity</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
<p>
    A particle P of mass m can slide on a smooth horizontal table.
    P is connected to a light inextensible string which passes through a small
    smooth hole O in the table, so that the lower end of the string hangs 
    vertically below the table while P moves on top with the string
    taut (see figure)
</p>

<p>
    Initially the lower end of the string is held fixed with P moving with speed
    <m>u</m> on a circle of radius <m>a</m>.
    The string is now pulled down from below in such a way that the length of
    the string <m>r(t)</m> decreases at a constant rate <m>V</m>.
</p>


    <image source="images\activities\lect17\rope_hole.png">
    <shortdescription></shortdescription>
    </image>


<p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Does the momentum of the particle stay constant?
                If not, what external forces act on it?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Does the kinetic energy of the particle stay constant?
                If not, what does work on it?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Does the angular momentum of the particle about the hole remain
                constant?
                If not, what is the net torque on the particle?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Find the speed of the particle as a function of time.
                Reminder: <m>~{r(0)=a}</m>, <m>~{v(0)=u}</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


<exercise>
<p>
    We looked at a pendulum some time ago using energy methods, and that's still
    the best way to look at it, but we can use the pendulum to get some practice
    using angular momentum.
    To fix our coordinates, let's take the angle <m>\phi</m> to be positive when
    the string is rotated counterclockwise with respect to the vertical.
    This corresponds to taking the positive direction for angular velocity and
    angular momentum to be <alert>out of the page</alert> (check with your right
    hand).
    We'll call this the <m>z</m> direction.
</p>

<image width="50%" source="images\activities\lect17\pendulum.png">
<shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
</image>
<p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Find the vector component <m>\tau_{z}</m> of the net torque on
                the particle about the pivot point (a in the figure) as a
                function of the angle <m>\phi</m>.
                Note: I'm asking for a vector component, so signs are important.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Check that your equation also gives the correct vector component
                when <m>~{\phi &lt; 0}</m>, that is, if the string is swinging to
                the left instead of to the right as shown here.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Since your solution works for all <m>\phi</m>, we can use
                <m>\tau_{z} = \dfrac{dL_z}{dt}</m> without fear.
                Use this to find <m>\ddot{\phi}</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</p>
</exercise>

</worksheet>